WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.175
A SignalR hub is not just a convenient
transport wrapper.

2
00:00:06.255 --> 00:00:11.221
Its method names, argument lists, return
values,

3
00:00:11.301 --> 00:00:17.567
and side effects are part of the
application security surface.

4
00:00:17.647 --> 00:00:24.386
If a method changes application state,
looks up data, joins a group,

5
00:00:24.466 --> 00:00:30.023
sends a targeted notification, or triggers
a workflow,

6
00:00:30.103 --> 00:00:38.379
then it should be treated as an
intentional application action with a defined trust

7
00:00:38.459 --> 00:00:39.523
boundary.

8
00:00:39.603 --> 00:00:43.977
Good hub design starts with narrow
methods.

9
00:00:44.057 --> 00:00:49.141
A hub method should represent one clear
operation,

10
00:00:49.221 --> 00:00:57.497
accept the smallest useful set of inputs,
and return only the data the caller needs.

11
00:00:57.577 --> 00:01:06.090
Broad methods that accept loosely shaped
objects or leave important interpretation

12
00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:13.145
to the client are harder to review and
harder to protect consistently.

13
00:01:13.225 --> 00:01:18.073
Client callbacks need a different mental
model.

14
00:01:18.153 --> 00:01:25.483
A server may call client-side functions to
deliver data, update a screen,

15
00:01:25.563 --> 00:01:29.228
or notify a user about a state change.

16
00:01:29.308 --> 00:01:33.564
Those callbacks are delivery mechanisms.

17
00:01:33.644 --> 00:01:41.093
They should not be the only place where
business rules, permission checks,

18
00:01:41.173 --> 00:01:47.557
tenant separation, or administrative
decisions are enforced.

19
00:01:47.637 --> 00:01:55.322
A modified or outdated client must not be
able to turn a display decision into a

20
00:01:55.402 --> 00:01:59.304
server-side authorization decision.

21
00:01:59.384 --> 00:02:04.822
Hub context, connection identifiers,
users, groups,

22
00:02:04.902 --> 00:02:12.114
and client proxies are useful, but they
should be handled deliberately.

23
00:02:12.194 --> 00:02:18.342
Separate public notifications,
authenticated user events,

24
00:02:18.422 --> 00:02:23.743
tenant-scoped messages, administrative
messages,

25
00:02:23.823 --> 00:02:27.488
and system operations in the design.

26
00:02:27.568 --> 00:02:34.189
That separation makes it easier to review
who can call each method,

27
00:02:34.269 --> 00:02:42.190
who can receive each message, and what
server-side data supports the decision.

28
00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.935
A secure SignalR API is predictable.

29
00:02:46.015 --> 00:02:50.626
The method set is small enough to reason
about,

30
00:02:50.706 --> 00:02:55.435
sensitive operations stand out during
review,

31
00:02:55.515 --> 00:03:03.437
and the server remains responsible for
identity, authorization, validation,

32
00:03:03.517 --> 00:03:07.300
routing decisions, and audit events.
